READING_LINEAGE=Each review brief is itself a declared ledger path and therefore contributes exactly one omit-mutation parametrisation, so both figures rise by 1 per added brief. Round 1 (291 files): 1585 / 317, independently re-run by three routes at 179.21s / 182.66s / 187.27s. Round 2 (292 files, + .r6-round2-evidence-authenticity-task.md): 1586 / 318, independently re-confirmed by the round-2 route at 174.06s / 39.75s. Round 2b (293 files, + .r6-round2b-blocking-fix-verify-task.md): 1587 / 319 as recorded above. Both figures are always updated together — the round-2 BLOCKING was caused by updating FIVE_GATES alone and leaving FULL_NOBUILD_PYTEST stale.
READING_CALIBER_NOTE=An earlier draft of this sidecar recorded 1583/315. Those were captured after the topology-ledger fix but BEFORE .r6-final-review-task.md and .r6-freeze-evidence.sha256 were themselves declared in the ledgers. Each of those two files contributes exactly one omit-mutation parametrisation to test_r5_topology_gate.py::test_mutation_omit_each_added_path_calls_formal_predicate, hence +2 on both the full run and the five gates. Verified by collect-only: the sidecar appears in 1 case and the task brief in 1 case. Neither file affects the workspace identity hash because .r6-freeze-evidence.sha256 is in EXCLUDES and the task brief was already counted at freeze time. Both figures above are the post-declaration steady state, cross-confirmed by three independent execution contexts.
